// Add new story objects to this array. Keep each retelling in your own wording.
window.TALEATLAS_STORIES = [
{id:'lion-mouse',title:'The Lion and the Mouse',region:'Ancient Greece',country:'Greece',theme:'Kindness',readingTime:'3 min',summary:'A tiny mouse discovers that even the smallest friend can make a mighty difference.',moral:'Kindness is never wasted.',leftPage:'A lion was sleeping beneath a shady tree when a little mouse hurried across his paw. The lion woke with a roar and caught the frightened creature. “Please let me go,” squeaked the mouse. “One day, I may help you.” The lion laughed at the thought of such a tiny animal helping him, but he opened his paw and let the mouse run free.',rightPage:'Some days later, hunters caught the lion in a strong net. He tugged and growled, but the ropes held tight. Hearing his cries, the mouse hurried over. With sharp little teeth, it gnawed through one rope, then another, until the lion could step out. The lion thanked his small friend and understood that strength comes in many forms.'},
{id:'monkey-crocodile',title:'The Monkey and the Crocodile',region:'South Asia',country:'India',theme:'Wisdom',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'A quick-thinking monkey finds a way out of a dangerous river crossing.',moral:'A calm mind can find a way through trouble.',leftPage:'Beside a wide river stood a fruit tree full of sweet, red fruit. A monkey lived among its branches and often tossed fruit to a crocodile resting below. Before long, the two became friends. One day, the crocodile invited the monkey to visit his home across the water. The monkey climbed onto his back, and they set off.',rightPage:'Halfway across, the crocodile admitted that he had been sent to bring the monkey to his family for a cruel purpose. The monkey felt afraid, but kept his voice calm. “I left my heart in the tree,” he said. “Take me back so I can fetch it.” The crocodile turned around. As soon as they reached the bank, the monkey sprang safely into the branches. He told the crocodile that a friend should never be tricked.'},
{id:'momotaro',title:'Momotaro, the Peach Boy',region:'East Asia',country:'Japan',theme:'Courage',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'A child found inside a giant peach grows up to help a village in need.',moral:'Courage grows stronger when friends work together.',leftPage:'Long ago in Japan, an elderly couple lived beside a river. One day, the woman found an enormous peach floating downstream. When she and her husband opened it, a healthy baby boy appeared inside. They named him Momotaro, which means Peach Boy, and raised him with great love. As he grew, he became brave, thoughtful, and strong.',rightPage:'When Momotaro heard that ogres were troubling the nearby islands, he decided to help. His parents packed him millet dumplings for the journey. Along the road, a dog, a monkey, and a pheasant joined him after he shared his food. Together they crossed the sea, faced the ogres, and asked them to stop frightening people. The companions returned home with the stolen treasures and were welcomed as heroes.'},
{id:'anansi-wisdom',title:'Anansi and the Pot of Wisdom',region:'Africa',country:'Ghana and Akan traditions',theme:'Wisdom',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'Anansi tries to keep all wisdom for himself and learns why it should be shared.',moral:'Wisdom grows when people share it.',leftPage:'Anansi the spider once gathered all the world’s wisdom into a pot. He decided to keep it for himself, certain that nobody else deserved to know so much. He planned to hide the pot high in a tree, where no one could reach it. He tied it in front of himself and began to climb, but the pot kept bumping against the trunk.',rightPage:'Anansi’s young son watched and suggested tying the pot on his back instead. The simple advice worked at once. Anansi stopped climbing and stared at the pot. If his own child had thought of something he had missed, then wisdom could not belong to him alone. In frustration, he dropped the pot, and it broke. In the story, wisdom spread through the world, where everyone could learn and contribute.'},
{id:'tortoise-hare',title:'The Tortoise and the Hare',region:'Ancient Greece',country:'Greece',theme:'Perseverance',readingTime:'3 min',summary:'A speedy hare learns not to underestimate a steady tortoise.',moral:'Steady effort can beat careless confidence.',leftPage:'A hare loved to boast about how fast he could run. One day, he teased a tortoise for moving so slowly. The tortoise calmly challenged him to a race. The other animals gathered to watch, and the race began. The hare dashed far ahead and looked back. The tortoise was still moving, one careful step after another.',rightPage:'Certain he would win, the hare decided to rest beneath a tree and soon fell asleep. The tortoise did not stop. He kept going along the path, past the tree, and toward the finish line. When the hare finally woke, he raced as fast as he could, but the tortoise had already arrived. The animals cheered for the tortoise, whose steady effort had carried him to the end.'},
{id:'stone-soup',title:'Stone Soup',region:'Europe',country:'European oral traditions',theme:'Community',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'Travellers help a cautious village discover the joy of sharing.',moral:'Small contributions can make something wonderful together.',leftPage:'Three hungry travellers arrived in a village, but the villagers were worried about their own supplies and offered no food. The travellers filled a pot with water, placed a clean stone inside, and set it over a fire. They announced that they were making stone soup. Curious villagers came to watch. “It would taste even better with a carrot,” one traveller said.',rightPage:'A villager brought a carrot. Another added potatoes, and someone else brought onions and herbs. Soon the pot smelled delicious. Everyone contributed a little, and the travellers shared the soup with the whole village. The people discovered that they had enough when they worked together. Different versions of this traditional tale are told in many places, but its warm message remains familiar.'},
{id:'bremen-musicians',title:'The Bremen Town Musicians',region:'Europe',country:'Germany',theme:'Friendship',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'Four animals who feel unwanted discover that they are stronger together.',moral:'Friends can help one another find a new beginning.',leftPage:'An old donkey set out for the town of Bremen, hoping to become a musician. On the road, he met a tired dog, a cat, and a rooster. Each had left home because someone thought they were no longer useful. The donkey invited them to join him. They travelled together, sharing the road and encouraging one another.',rightPage:'At night, they saw a house where robbers were feasting. The four friends climbed on one another and made the loudest, strangest music they could. Startled by the noise and shadows, the robbers fled into the forest. The animals settled into the cosy house, found food and shelter, and stayed together. Their journey had led them somewhere better than they had imagined.'},
{id:'boy-wolf',title:'The Boy Who Cried Wolf',region:'Ancient Greece',country:'Greece',theme:'Honesty',readingTime:'3 min',summary:'A shepherd boy learns that trust is hard to rebuild once it is lost.',moral:'People need to be able to trust your words.',leftPage:'A young shepherd watched a flock of sheep on a hillside. One quiet afternoon, he grew bored and shouted, “Wolf! A wolf is chasing the sheep!” The villagers hurried up the hill to help, only to discover that the boy had played a trick. He laughed at their worried faces. A few days later, he played the same trick again, and the villagers came running once more.',rightPage:'Then a wolf really did appear. The frightened boy called for help with all his strength. The villagers heard him, but remembered the earlier tricks and thought he was joking again. The boy managed to get away and learned a serious lesson: honesty matters even when telling the truth feels less exciting. The story reminds us that trust is built by being truthful again and again.'},
{id:'porridge-pot',title:'The Magic Porridge Pot',region:'Europe',country:'Germany; a version collected by the Brothers Grimm',theme:'Carefulness',readingTime:'3 min',summary:'A magical pot makes porridge, but someone must remember how to stop it.',moral:'A gift is best used with care.',leftPage:'A kind girl met an old woman in the forest and helped her. As a reward, the woman gave her a little pot and taught her the words that would make it cook sweet porridge. She also taught the girl the words to make it stop. At home, the girl and her mother were delighted. Whenever they were hungry, the pot made a warm meal.',rightPage:'One day, the girl was away and her mother tried to use the pot. She remembered how to start it, but forgot how to stop it. The porridge filled the pot, then the kitchen, then the road outside. When the girl returned, she said the stopping words, and at last the pot became still. The village had to eat its way through the porridge before everyone could pass along the road.'},
{id:'fisherman-wife',title:'The Fisherman and His Wife',region:'Europe',country:'Germany; a version collected by the Brothers Grimm',theme:'Contentment',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'A series of wishes shows how endless wanting can spoil simple happiness.',moral:'Gratitude helps us recognise what is already enough.',leftPage:'A fisherman caught a strange fish that spoke and begged to be released. The fisherman kindly returned it to the sea without asking for a reward. When he told his wife, she wished for a better home. The fisherman returned to the shore, called to the fish, and the wish came true. But soon his wife wanted more: a grander house, then a palace.',rightPage:'Each time, the fisherman felt uneasy but went back to the sea. Each wish was granted, yet his wife remained dissatisfied and asked for still more. At last, she wished to rule over everything. The fisherman called the fish one final time, and the wish was not granted. When he returned home, they were back in their old, simple hut. The tale invites readers to think about gratitude, fairness, and knowing when enough is enough.'},
{id:'clever-rabbit',title:'The Clever Rabbit and the Lion',region:'South Asia',country:'India; Panchatantra tradition',theme:'Problem-solving',readingTime:'3 min',summary:'A small rabbit protects the forest animals with a clever plan.',moral:'Thought and courage can be more useful than size.',leftPage:'A proud lion frightened the animals of the forest, who worried that he would hunt them all. The animals agreed that one would visit him each day, hoping to prevent a greater disaster. One day, it was the turn of a small rabbit. The rabbit travelled slowly and arrived late, saying that another lion had tried to stop him on the way.',rightPage:'The angry lion demanded to see this rival. The rabbit led him to a deep well and pointed down. The lion saw his own reflection in the water and heard his roar echo back. Believing another lion was inside, he leapt toward it and fell into the well. The animals were safe again. In this traditional story, the rabbit’s careful thinking succeeds where strength alone would not.'},
{id:'grateful-crane',title:'The Grateful Crane',region:'East Asia',country:'Japan',theme:'Gratitude',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'A kind act leads to a mysterious gift, and a promise must be respected.',moral:'Gratitude and respect matter more than curiosity.',leftPage:'On a snowy day, a poor man freed a crane caught in a trap. Later, a young visitor arrived at his home and asked to stay. The man welcomed the guest. The visitor began weaving beautiful cloth, asking only that nobody watch while the work was done. The cloth was so lovely that the man could sell it and live more comfortably.',rightPage:'Curiosity eventually overcame the man’s promise, and he looked inside the weaving room. There he discovered the visitor was the crane he had rescued, using its own feathers to make the cloth. The crane was saddened that the promise had been broken and had to leave. Versions of this Japanese tale vary, but many centre on kindness, gratitude, and respecting another person’s boundaries.'},
{id:"the-tortoise-with-a-pretty-daughter",title:"The Tortoise with a Pretty Daughter",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Adet’s joyful song brings the prince’s attention, but an unfair royal rule threatens to keep her from the celebration. Her kindness helps the king see that everyone belongs.",moral:"A celebration is brighter when everyone is welcome.",leftPage:"In a Southern Nigerian town, Tortoise loved his daughter Adet, whose bright smile made neighbors happy. The king had made a foolish rule: no girl said to be prettier than the prince’s palace portraits could join the spring celebration. When the prince heard Adet sing while helping carry baskets to the market, he invited her to the palace feast. Tortoise worried the king would turn them away.",rightPage:"At the feast, the king saw Adet waiting outside and asked what was wrong. She gently asked why a celebration should leave anyone out, then offered to teach the palace children her song. As they sang together, the king realized his rule was unfair and canceled it. Everyone joined the dance, and Tortoise went home proud: Adet had won hearts through her kindness."},
{id:"the-kings-magic-drum",title:"The King's Magic Drum",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Sharing",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A proud Tortoise borrows a king’s food-making drum, then learns that keeping a promise matters more than showing off.",moral:"A promise kept—and a meal shared—can make everyone feel welcome.",leftPage:"In Calabar, King Efriam’s drum could summon a splendid meal whenever people gathered. He lent it to Tortoise after a palm nut from Tortoise’s tree rolled to the king’s little daughter, and Tortoise asked for the drum instead of other gifts. The king explained its rule: never step over a stick or fallen branch while carrying it. Tortoise proudly invited everyone to supper.",rightPage:"As Tortoise carried the drum to impress his guests, he missed a branch on the path and stepped over it. The dishes turned sour, and the feast was spoiled. Tortoise admitted his mistake; the king kindly helped everyone share the simple food they had brought. Tortoise returned the drum and promised to watch his steps. At the next feast, he helped welcome every guest."},
{id:"ituen-and-the-kings-wife",title:"Ituen and the King’s Wife",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Sharing",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A resourceful young man helps a queen find a fair way to share market food, and his family’s honest explanation inspires a kinder custom.",moral:"Honest words and thoughtful sharing can turn worry into a better way for everyone.",leftPage:"In Calabar, young Ituen had little food, so he gathered leftover grain after market each day. One morning, Queen Attem’s helper noticed his bright smile and strong hands. The queen needed someone to help plan a fair way to share unsold food, and invited him to the palace. Ituen worried the king might misunderstand, but his mother said, “Go with honesty, and tell us what happens.”",rightPage:"At the palace, Ituen showed the queen how hungry families could receive food before scraps were swept away. The king arrived and wondered why a visitor had come secretly at night. Ituen explained everything, and his mother confirmed the truth. The king thanked them and opened a sharing table in the market; leftovers were set aside for dogs and birds too. From then on, no one had to search for a meal."},
{id:"of-the-pretty-stranger-who-killed-the-king",title:"The Pretty Stranger Who Killed the King",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Conflict",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A witch from Itu disguises herself as a beautiful stranger, kills King Mbotu, and helps Itu take Old Town by surprise.",moral:"War brings loss and grief to whole communities.",leftPage:"Mbotu, king of Old Town, had won many wars, taken prisoners, and made many enemies. The people of Itu wanted to defeat him but could not beat his army in battle, so they asked a witch for help. The witch changed herself into a beautiful young woman and hid a knife before traveling to Old Town. A celebration was underway, and everyone admired her. King Mbotu invited her to the palace and said he would marry her that day. She prepared his meal with medicine that made him sleepy. While he slept, she killed King Mbotu and carried proof of his death back to Itu.",rightPage:"The king of Itu gathered soldiers and secretly traveled by canoe through the creeks to Old Town. The people there discovered Mbotu’s death the next morning and began to mourn. While they were grieving, Itu’s army attacked Old Town by surprise. Without their leader or time to prepare, the people of Old Town were defeated; many were killed or taken prisoner. The people of Itu celebrated, while Old Town was left in grief."},
{id:"the-disobedient-daughter-who-married-a-skull",title:"Afiong and the Stranger with a Borrowed Face",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Listening",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Afiong follows a charming stranger to a faraway land, where a kind elder helps her return home and reconsider her choice.",moral:"Pause, listen to people who care for you, and take time to know someone before trusting them.",leftPage:"In Cobham Town, Afiong loved hearing that she was the loveliest girl around. Her parents asked her to consider kind young people they knew, but she only wanted to meet someone handsome and exciting. One day, a charming stranger appeared at the market. Afiong invited him home, and soon set out with him for his faraway country, despite her parents’ worried advice.",rightPage:"Across the border, the stranger’s borrowed good looks disappeared: he was a skull. Afiong felt afraid, but his gentle mother welcomed her. Afiong helped the old woman with daily chores, and the mother thanked her by sending her home on a soft breeze. Her parents rejoiced. Afiong listened to their counsel, made peace, and chose a kind companion from her own town."},
{id:"the-king-who-married-the-cocks-daughter",title:"The King Who Married the Cock’s Daughter",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When jealous guests trick the Cock’s daughter at a feast, the king learns to listen, apologize, and welcome her back as she is.",moral:"Be fair to others, and never judge someone for being different.",leftPage:"In Calabar, King Effiom married Adia, the Cock’s daughter, and admired her cheerful ways. Adia loved bright kernels of corn; her father had warned the king that she might peck them up. Some palace ladies, jealous of the king’s attention, scattered corn at a feast. Adia happily pecked a few kernels, and the guests began to giggle. Hurt and embarrassed, the king sent her home.",rightPage:"That evening, a kind courtier explained that jealous guests had planned the trick. The king saw he had judged Adia unfairly, and went to her father’s cottage to apologize. He invited Adia back, promising no more tricks or tests. Adia agreed, but asked that corn be shared with birds and people alike. From then on, the palace set out a bowl of grain in the courtyard. At palace feasts, no one was laughed at for being different."},
{id:"concerning-the-woman-the-ape-and-the-child",title:"Concerning the Woman, the Ape, and the Child",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Trust",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When a worried rumor makes a father fear his child’s ape friend, he takes time to see what is really happening. The family and neighbors find a kind, safe way to care for both child and ape.",moral:"A careful question can turn a fearful guess into understanding.",leftPage:"Nkoyo often brought her baby to the yam farm, where a gentle ape visited from the forest. He sat nearby, made funny faces, and fetched the baby’s bright wooden rattle when it rolled away. One afternoon, a passing hunter saw them together and hurried to tell the child’s father that the ape was carrying the baby off.",rightPage:"Okun felt worried, but Nkoyo asked him to come and look before deciding. They found the ape sitting beside the baby beneath a shady tree, returning the rattle whenever it slipped away. Okun thanked the ape with a basket of fruit, and the neighbors helped make a little fenced play space near the farm, where the family could visit their new friend safely."},
{id:"the-fish-and-the-leopards-wife",title:"The Fish and the Leopard’s Wife",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Honesty and friendship",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A fish breaks a promise while visiting his friends, then admits his mistake and makes things right.",moral:"Honesty helps repair trust, and being a good friend means respecting others’ homes.",leftPage:"Long ago in Calabar, Fish could walk on land and often visited his friend Leopard and Leopard’s wife. One day, while Leopard gathered firewood, Mrs. Leopard prepared a little plate of fruit for supper. Fish had promised not to touch it, but the sweet smell tempted him. He ate every piece, then felt ashamed when she found the empty plate.",rightPage:"When Leopard returned, Mrs. Leopard told him what happened, and Fish admitted the truth instead of making excuses. King Eyo listened and said that friendship needs honesty, and that a guest must respect another home. Fish apologized and helped gather fresh fruit. Since Fish loved cool water, he chose to live in the river, visiting the leopard family only when invited."},
{id:"why-the-worms-live-underneath-the-ground",title:"Why the Worms Live Underneath the Ground",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Staying Safe",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When boastful driver ants challenge the worms, the little creatures discover that the safest place for them is beneath the ground.",moral:"Knowing when to step away can be a wise kind of strength.",leftPage:"Long ago, King Eyo invited the creatures to a grand feast. Afterward, the head driver ant stood up and bragged that no one could match his people’s strength. He laughed at the worms for being small and wiggly. The worms felt hurt, so the king proposed a contest to settle the argument, and everyone gathered to watch.",rightPage:"When the ants marched toward the meeting place in a great dark ribbon, the worms saw how powerful a crowd they made. Rather than risk a frightening clash, the worms slipped safely beneath the soft earth. King Eyo praised their clever choice and asked the ants to stop boasting. Ever since, worms have made their home underground, coming up after rain and ducking below when danger draws near."},
{id:"why-the-sun-and-the-moon-live-in-the-sky",title:"Why the Sun and the Moon Live in the Sky",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"The Sun and Moon welcome their friend Water, whose many companions fill their home until they find a new place in the sky.",moral:"Good friends make room for one another—and adapt when plans change.",leftPage:"Long ago, the Sun and Water were friends, though Water had never visited the Sun’s home. When the Sun asked why, Water explained that his many companions—fish, frogs, and flowing streams—would need a very wide place to gather. The Sun promised to build a spacious courtyard. With Moon’s help, he prepared it and invited Water to visit the next day.",rightPage:"Water arrived with his lively crowd, and soon the courtyard brimmed. The Sun checked that everyone was comfortable, but the guests kept arriving; the friendly hosts climbed onto the roof to stay dry. As the water rose higher, Sun and Moon floated safely upward into the open sky. There they found a bright home above the clouds, while Water and his creatures filled the rivers and sea below."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-lightning-and-the-thunder",title:"The Story of the Lightning and the Thunder",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Listening",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A quick-tempered ram and his mother are asked to leave town, and the king finds them a home in the sky.",moral:"A pause to listen can help keep others safe.",leftPage:"Long ago, Thunder, a mother sheep, and Lightning, her restless ram, lived near a busy town. Whenever Lightning grew cross, he flashed across roofs and trees, startling everyone. His mother called him to pause, but he rushed on. At last, the neighbors asked their king for help, worried that a careless flash might reach their homes or gardens.",rightPage:"The king first sent them far from town, but Lightning’s quick flashes still made people uneasy. So the king gave them a home high in the sky, above houses and fields. There, Thunder could call after her son whenever he darted ahead. Lightning learned to keep his bright flashes among the clouds, and people below saw the light, then heard Thunder’s rumbling reminder to be careful. From then on, they watched storms from safe shelter."},
{id:"why-dead-people-are-buried",title:"The Message Dog Forgot",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Listening",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A forgotten message and a sheep’s mixed-up words explain how people came to bury their loved ones.",moral:"Listen carefully, and take care when passing a message along.",leftPage:"In the first days, people and animals lived near the Creator, who hoped that those who died would soon rise again. He asked Dog, his swift messenger, to tell everyone: “Lay your loved one in the family yard, cover the place with cool ashes, and wait one day.” Dog hurried off, but a tempting bone made him stop—and the important words slipped from his mind.",rightPage:"When Dog did not return, Creator sent Sheep with the same message. Sheep nibbled grass and remembered only the final instruction: “Place the departed gently beneath the earth.” The people trusted Sheep’s words and began burying their loved ones. When Dog returned with the first message, they had already chosen their custom. Creator saw their kindness, and Dog practiced remembering each message before setting out again."},
{id:"of-the-fat-woman-who-melted-away",title:"The Woman Made of Palm Oil",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A woman who melts in the sun is pressured to work outdoors, but her sister and household find a caring, fair solution.",moral:"Listen to one another and share work fairly, with care for each person’s needs.",leftPage:"In a Southern Nigerian village, a woman made of palm oil was known as the Fat Woman. She stayed in the shade because sunshine could make her melt. At her new home, another wife grew cross at carrying all the firewood and water. When she urged the Fat Woman into the sunny field, her little sister warned her to stay beneath a tree—but wanting peace, the Fat Woman stepped into the sun.",rightPage:"She became a small golden puddle beneath a leaf, and her sister carefully gathered it into a cool, covered pot. When the husband returned, he listened to both women and helped them agree: chores would be shared, and the Fat Woman would work only in shade. After three months, the sister opened the pot; the Fat Woman stood smiling, whole again. The household welcomed her and began its fairer routine together."},
{id:"the-king-and-the-ju-ju-tree",title:"The King and the Ju Ju Tree",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Respect for nature",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"King Udo learns that working with the Ju Ju tree protects both the spring and the people who depend on it.",moral:"When we respect nature and listen before acting, we can find a solution that helps everyone.",leftPage:"When King Udo returned to the spring behind his house, he found a great Ju Ju tree spreading across the path. “Clear it away,” he ordered, but every cut closed again. The king tried once himself, and a tiny twig brushed his eye, leaving it sore. His daughter suggested they ask the tree why it guarded the spring.",rightPage:"A gentle healer rinsed the king’s eye, then helped his daughter listen beneath the leaves. The tree’s roots, it seemed, were holding the spring’s banks together. The king apologized and asked the villagers to make a narrow path without harming the roots. The tree lifted one branch, revealing clear water. From then on, the king and his people cared for the spring together."},
{id:"of-the-pretty-girl-and-the-seven-jealous-women",title:"Akim and the River Guardian",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Akim’s jealous companions leave her without the customary gift for the river guardian, but a little bird tells the truth and helps her family bring her home. Their town turns jealousy into a chance to make things right.",moral:"Jealousy can hurt others; honesty and kindness help mend what has gone wrong.",leftPage:"Akim, a kind young woman from Ibibio, was invited by seven companions to a lively town gathering. Though they envied her, she went with them, hoping to enjoy the music. At the river, the others offered gifts to its guardian, as was the custom, but they had not shared any with Akim. The guardian gently carried her beneath the water to a quiet, safe place.",rightPage:"A little bird had seen everything, and told Akim’s worried parents the truth. They brought traditional gifts to the river, and the guardian returned Akim safely to shore. At a gathering, the seven companions admitted what they had done. The town asked them to apologize and help Akim prepare the next celebration. They worked together, and soon the music and friendship returned."},
{id:"the-lucky-fisherman",title:"The Lucky Fisherman",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A patient fisherman studies the river, finds a better place for his traps, and shares what he learns with his neighbors.",moral:"Careful patience and sharing can help a whole community thrive.",leftPage:"Long ago, beside a river in Southern Nigeria, Akon Obo had little to spare. He wove fish baskets from bamboo-palm strips and baited them with palm nuts, but his first catches were small. Rather than give up, he watched the water each morning. One day he spotted tiny ripples beneath a shaded bend and guessed the fish were gathering in a deeper pool.",rightPage:"Akon carefully set his baskets near the bend. Soon they held enough fish for his family, with more to sell at the market. When neighbors asked how he had found such a good spot, he showed them how to watch the river and weave sturdy traps. Before long, many homes had food to share. They called Akon the Lucky Fisherman, and he smiled: a little patience had brought luck to everyone."},
{id:"the-orphan-boy-and-the-magic-stone",title:"The Orphan Boy and the Magic Stone",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Generosity",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"After losing his father, Ayong finds a magic stone that can grant wishes. When it is time to return it, he chooses to share what he has gained and help his home flourish.",moral:"A gift becomes more meaningful when we use it to help others.",leftPage:"Ayong Kita was still young when his father died, and trouble at home sent him into the green forest alone. Hungry and worried, he remembered a dream in which his father had shown him where treasure lay. An old woman kindly guided him away from unsafe water, then gave him a shining stone and told him how its wishes could help him find a safe home.",rightPage:"Ayong found the treasure and used the stone to build a welcoming home, where neighbors could gather and share meals. Years later, the old woman reminded him that the stone belonged in the stream where she had found it. Ayong returned it with thanks, then used his own good fortune to help his home village thrive. His neighbors trusted him because he cared for them."},
{id:"concerning-the-fate-of-essido-and-his-evil-companions",title:"Eyo and Essido’s New Beginning",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Essido squanders his inheritance and listens to companions who urge him to betray his generous brother. A last moment of honesty opens a better path for them both.",moral:"Good friends help us choose what is right; honesty can turn us toward a fresh start.",leftPage:"In a riverside town lived two brothers, Eyo and Essido. When their father died, kind Eyo shared his inheritance with Essido. But Essido spent his share on grand parties, while Eyo worked steadily trading yams and fish. Soon Essido had nothing left. His companions, who liked the feasts, urged him to borrow more and run away with them to another town.",rightPage:"There, the companions soon spent the borrowed money too. They suggested a wicked plan: Essido should pretend to mend his ways, then secretly harm Eyo to take his wealth. Remembering his brother’s kindness, Essido hurried home and confessed the plan before anyone was hurt. Eyo forgave him but asked him to work honestly. Essido agreed, and the brothers traded together, sharing fair profits and welcoming kinder friends."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-drummer-and-the-alligators",title:"The Story of the Drummer and the Alligators",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Courage and kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A devoted mother seeks help when her gifted drummer son is taken by the alligators, and a town meeting brings the truth to light.",moral:"Courage and care can help bring the truth to light.",leftPage:"Long ago beside Calabar River lived Affiong and her son Edet, who could make a drum sing like rain on leaves. When jealous youths lured him toward the water, alligators carried him to their hidden hall. Affiong did not give up. She asked the clever river guide Ininen for help, and together they watched the tide and planned a quiet rescue.",rightPage:"At low tide, Ininen guided Edet home; shaken and silent, the boy slowly found his voice again with his mother’s care. They kept a clue from the hidden hall and brought it to a town meeting. When the youths recognized it, they admitted their wrong and promised to stop frightening people. The chiefs set them to helping mend canoes, and Edet played his drum at a joyful riverside dance."},
{id:"the-election-of-the-king-bird",title:"The Election of the King Bird",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Leadership",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When the birds cannot agree on a leader, the calm fishing eagle helps them notice one another’s strengths and is chosen as king bird.",moral:"A good leader listens calmly and helps everyone’s gifts shine.",leftPage:"Long ago, King Essiya needed a leader for every kind of bird, but the birds all claimed the honor. Hawks bragged about speed, tall waders about their long legs, and tiny forest birds could hardly be heard above the commotion. Essiya invited them to gather by the river and asked them to show how they could help keep the bird world in harmony. Then everyone began talking at once.",rightPage:"While the others swooped and squabbled, Ituen, the black-and-white fishing eagle, stayed high in a tree, watching patiently. When the king asked him to speak, he spread his wings and called for quiet, then pointed out how each bird had a useful gift: speed, careful steps, keen eyes, or a gentle song. The birds listened and settled down. Essiya named Ituen king bird, to lead by courage and calm sense."},
{id:"the-wolf-and-the-lamb",title:"The Wolf and the Lamb",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Fairness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A wolf invents unfair complaints against a lamb by a stream, but the lamb’s call brings help and safety.",moral:"A calm, truthful answer matters, and unfairness should not go unchallenged.",leftPage:"On a sunny hillside in Ancient Greece, a young lamb paused beside a clear stream for a drink. A wolf appeared on the opposite bank and complained, “You are muddling my water!” The lamb gently replied, “The stream flows from you toward me, so my sip cannot cloud the water above you.” The wolf frowned, searching for another reason to scold.",rightPage:"“You once spoke unkindly of me,” he growled. “I was only born this spring,” said the lamb. The wolf had no fair answer, but the lamb called loudly for the shepherd. Hearing the call, the shepherd came over and led the little lamb safely home. The wolf slipped away, while the stream went on sparkling for everyone."},
{id:"the-ass-and-the-grasshopper",title:"The Ass and the Grasshopper",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"An ass tries to sing like the grasshoppers by living on dew, but dies of hunger.",moral:"What nourishes one creature may not nourish another.",leftPage:"An ass heard grasshoppers chirping and was enchanted by their song. He asked what food gave them such lovely voices. “We live on dew,” they replied. The ass decided he would eat nothing but dew too, hoping to learn to sing like them.",rightPage:"The ass continued eating only dew and had no other food. Before long, he grew weak and died of hunger. The grasshoppers kept chirping in the grass. Their diet suited them, but it could not keep an ass healthy."},
{id:"the-wolf-and-the-crane",title:"The Wolf and the Crane",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Caution",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A crane removes a bone from a wolf’s throat, but he refuses to pay her promised fee.",moral:"When you help someone dangerous, do not expect a reward; be glad to escape safely.",leftPage:"A wolf had a bone stuck in his throat. He hired a crane to reach in with her long beak and pull it out, promising to pay her well. The crane carefully did the job and drew her head back safely. Then she asked for the payment the wolf had promised.",rightPage:"Wolf refused to pay the promised fee. He told Crane she should be glad she escaped his jaws unharmed. The crane flew away safely, but received no reward. She had trusted a dangerous wolf and was fortunate to get out of danger."},
{id:"the-father-and-his-sons",title:"The Father and His Sons",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Working Together",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A father helps his quarrelsome sons discover that they are stronger when they work together.",moral:"When we cooperate, we can do more than we can alone.",leftPage:"In a sunny village in Greece, a father’s three sons argued over every small chore. One morning, he asked them to bring a handful of slender sticks from the garden. Each son insisted his way of gathering them was best, and soon the sticks lay scattered while the brothers huffed at one another. Their father smiled and invited them to try a little test.",rightPage:"He tied the sticks into a neat bundle and let each son try to bend it; none could. Then he untied it and handed them one stick at a time, which each snapped easily. The brothers understood: alone, each could do only a little, but together they were sturdy. They gathered the sticks side by side and planned their chores as a team."},
{id:"the-bat-and-the-weasels",title:"The Bat and the Weasels",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A quick-thinking bat finds a peaceful way past two weasels who each mistake him for someone they dislike.",moral:"When trouble changes, pause and use what makes you unique.",leftPage:"At twilight, Pip the bat fluttered down to rest beside an olive tree. A weasel spotted him and blocked the path. “I never let a bird pass,” she declared. Pip kept his voice calm. “I’m not a bird,” he said. “Look at my soft fur and tiny paws—I’m a bat, more like a little mouse.” The weasel blinked, then let him go.",rightPage:"Pip hurried along, but another weasel stopped him near the garden wall. “Mice must turn back!” she warned. Pip thought quickly. “You’re right that I have paws,” he said, “but see these wide wings? I’m a bat, and the evening sky is my home.” The weasel stepped aside. Pip flew safely home, glad he had stayed calm and noticed what each moment called for."},
{id:"the-cock-and-the-jewel",title:"The Cock and the Jewel",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"What We Need",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A hungry rooster finds a shining jewel, but chooses useful grain to share with his hens.",moral:"Something beautiful is not always what we need most.",leftPage:"At sunrise, a rooster named Kiko scratched the courtyard earth, hoping to find breakfast for himself and his hens. Suddenly something bright winked beneath a leaf. He uncovered a jewel, smooth and sparkling like a drop of sunlight. “What a treasure!” he clucked, then wondered whether it might be something his hungry flock could eat.",rightPage:"He carried the jewel to the hens, who admired its colors but could not peck it into breakfast. Nearby lay one small barley grain he had missed. The rooster chose the grain and shared it with his friends. As they ate, he set the jewel safely beside the path, where its owner might find it. It shone beautifully, but a meal was what they needed most."},
{id:"the-swallow-and-the-crow",title:"The Swallow and the Crow",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Being prepared",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A swallow and a crow compare their feathers, then discover that bright colors and practical warmth each have their place.",moral:"Looks may catch the eye, but steady, useful qualities matter when the weather changes.",leftPage:"One bright spring morning, a Swallow swooped over a garden, showing off her smooth, shining feathers. “Just look at my lovely colors!” she chirped. A Crow nearby admired them, but soon the two birds began comparing their plumage. The Swallow thought beauty was the finest thing a bird could have; the Crow quietly wondered what those bright feathers would do when chilly days arrived.",rightPage:"A cool wind soon swept through the garden. The Swallow tucked herself into a snug, sunny nook, while the Crow’s thick, dark feathers kept her cozy on a branch. “Your feathers are splendid for spring,” said the Crow, “and mine are useful in the cold.” The Swallow smiled. They agreed that each bird had what she needed, and stopped arguing about whose feathers were best."},
{id:"the-dog-and-the-shadow",title:"The Dog and the Shadow",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Being Content",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A dog mistakes his reflection for another dog with a larger snack and learns to value what he already has.",moral:"Be grateful for what you have instead of reaching for an illusion.",leftPage:"On a bright morning, a small dog named Niko trotted home across a stone bridge with a piece of bread he had been given. In the clear stream below, another dog seemed to carry an even bigger piece. Niko paused, puzzled: the stranger’s bread looked like a feast, and he wondered whether he could somehow trade for it.",rightPage:"Niko leaned closer and opened his mouth to call. His own bread slipped gently into the shallow water and floated to the bank, while the other dog vanished with the ripples: it had only been his reflection. Niko retrieved his bread, brushed off a few drops, and continued home, glad to enjoy what he already had instead of chasing an illusion."},
{id:"hercules-and-the-wagoner",title:"Hercules and the Wagoner",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Helping Yourself",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When a wagon gets stuck in a muddy rut, its driver learns that asking for help must go hand in hand with doing his own best.",moral:"Ask for help when you need it, and remember to do your part, too.",leftPage:"One bright morning, a wagoner was taking a load of apples along a country lane. After a night of rain, one wheel sank into a muddy rut, and the wagon would not budge. The wagoner tugged at the reins, then sat beside the road and called to Hercules, the strong hero, asking him to come and move the wagon.",rightPage:"A calm voice answered, “I can guide you, but you must begin. Place your shoulder by the wheel, ask the oxen to pull, and push with all your strength.” The wagoner stood up and tried. The wheel rolled free, and the apples continued safely down the lane. Smiling, he thanked Hercules and remembered to take the first helpful step himself."},
{id:"the-mole-and-his-mother",title:"The Mole and His Mother",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Knowing Your Strengths",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A young mole boasts that he can see, but a gentle test helps him recognize both his limits and his other ways of learning.",moral:"It is wiser to be honest about what we can do and curious about what we can learn.",leftPage:"Under a warm Greek hillside, a young mole told his mother, “I can see just as well as anyone!” Mother did not argue. She set a tiny pinch of fragrant frankincense on a smooth stone and asked, “What have I placed here?” The mole leaned close, felt the stone, and guessed, “A little pebble.” He waited proudly for her answer.",rightPage:"“It is frankincense,” said Mother kindly. “Your eyes cannot see it, but your nose can help you discover it.” She let him sniff the sweet, woody scent, then invited him to find a nearby herb by smell. This time he named it correctly. The young mole smiled: he could not see, but he had other ways to learn—and no need to pretend."},
{id:"the-fawn-and-his-mother",title:"The Fawn and His Mother",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A curious fawn asks why his swift, strong mother still feels afraid of barking dogs. Together, they find a calm way to face her worry and choose a safe path.",moral:"Being brave does not mean never feeling afraid; it means finding a wise way forward, one small step at a time.",leftPage:"One bright morning, a young fawn watched his mother leap easily over a fallen branch. “You are swift and strong,” he said. “Why do you hurry away when dogs bark in the distance?” Just then, a sharp woof echoed from beyond the meadow. Mother’s ears lifted, and she began to tremble. The fawn saw that her fear was real, even though she was powerful.",rightPage:"“I cannot always stop myself from feeling afraid,” Mother admitted. The fawn stood beside her, and they took slow breaths beneath a broad oak while the distant dog’s bark faded. When the path grew quiet, Mother thanked him for staying near. They walked home together, keeping safely to the meadow trail; she still felt a flutter, but now knew she could pause and choose what to do."},
{id:"the-ass-the-fox-and-the-lion",title:"The Ass, the Fox, and the Lion",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Trust and honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"An Ass spots a Fox’s secret plan and uses a loud bray to escape danger, learning to choose honest companions.",moral:"Choose friends who keep their promises, and think carefully when something feels wrong.",leftPage:"On a warm morning in ancient Greece, an Ass and a Fox agreed to travel together and look out for each other. Soon they met a Lion resting beside the path. The Fox quietly offered to lead the Ass into a hollow, hoping to win the Lion’s favor. But the Ass heard the whisper and noticed the Fox’s eyes darting toward him.",rightPage:"“Friends don’t make secret plans that put one another in danger,” said the Ass. He stamped and gave a loud bray that echoed through the olive trees. The Lion, startled by the sudden noise, stepped back, and the Ass trotted safely toward a nearby farm. The Fox was left alone with the Lion and hurried away, wishing he had chosen honesty. From then on, the Ass picked companions who kept their promises."},
{id:"the-flies-and-the-honey-pot",title:"The Flies and the Honey-Pot",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A group of flies find a spilled honey jar and learn that a little patience can keep a sweet treat from becoming a sticky problem.",moral:"Enjoy good things thoughtfully; a little care can help everyone stay safe.",leftPage:"One warm morning, a honey jar slipped from a shelf and tipped onto a broad plate in the kitchen. A dozen curious flies soon discovered the golden puddle. They swooped down for a taste, laughing at their lucky find. But when they tried to lift off again, their feet clung to the sticky honey, and their wings could not flutter freely.",rightPage:"A little fly named Pip called, “Let’s stop tugging and ask for help!” Their buzzing reached Lina, the kind kitchen helper. She gently placed a clean leaf beside the plate, then used a spoon to loosen each fly and set it on the leaf. After resting in the sunshine, they flew away—this time taking turns at a safe drop of honey on a flower."},
{id:"the-farmer-and-the-snake",title:"The Farmer and the Snake",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Kindness and Caution",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"On a frosty morning, a farmer helps a cold snake warm up, then safely guides it back to the wild.",moral:"Be kind, but remember to treat wild creatures with care and keep a safe distance.",leftPage:"One frosty morning, a farmer found a snake curled beneath a hedge, too cold to move. He felt sorry for it and carried it toward his cottage. Then he paused: a wild snake might be frightened when it woke. Instead of bringing it indoors, he placed it gently in a sunny patch beside a warm stone and watched from a few steps away.",rightPage:"As the sun warmed the stone, the snake lifted its head and flicked its tongue. Startled by the farmer nearby, it gave a warning hiss. The farmer stepped back and used a long branch to clear a path toward the meadow. The snake glided into the grass unharmed, and the farmer went home glad he had helped without forgetting to be careful."},
{id:"the-man-and-the-lion",title:"The Man and the Lion",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Fairness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A traveler and a lion debate who is stronger, then discover that a statue may tell only its maker’s side of the story.",moral:"Before deciding what is true, listen to more than one side.",leftPage:"One bright morning, a traveler and a lion walked along a forest path, each sure that his kind was strongest. The traveler pointed to his broad shoulders; the lion showed his powerful paws. Their friendly boasting grew louder, but neither could prove his claim. At a clearing, they found a stone statue showing a man standing proudly above a lion.",rightPage:"The traveler smiled. “There—you see, a man has overcome a lion!” The lion studied the carving and answered, “A person made this statue. If lions carved stone, perhaps it would show things another way.” The traveler paused, then laughed at himself. They continued down the path, agreeing that one picture cannot tell the whole story—and that every side deserves a voice."},
{id:"the-mountain-in-labor",title:"The Mountain in Labor",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Surprises",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A rumbling mountain draws a worried crowd, but its surprising visitor is very small.",moral:"A great deal of noise may lead to a small surprise; wait and see before you worry.",leftPage:"One morning in ancient Greece, a tall mountain began to rumble. Pebbles hopped, and a soft puff of dust drifted from its side. Shepherds paused, birds fluttered into the sky, and soon people hurried over from nearby villages. “Something enormous must be happening,” they whispered, gathering at a safe distance to see what the mountain would do.",rightPage:"They waited and waited. At last, a tiny mouse poked its nose from a little hole, whiskers twitching, then scurried across the grass. The mountain grew quiet. The villagers smiled at their worried guesses and let the mouse go on its way. What sounded like a grand event had ended with one small visitor—and no disaster at all."},
{id:"the-bear-and-the-fox",title:"The Bear and the Fox",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A boastful bear says he is considerate of people when they are far away. A fox reminds him that true kindness matters when others are right there, too.",moral:"Kindness counts most when we show it to those around us.",leftPage:"One bright morning in an olive grove, Bear spotted Fox and began praising his own good manners. “I’m so considerate of people,” he declared, “that I never bother them when they are tucked away in their homes.” Fox listened, while a family of villagers came along the path carrying baskets of olives and bread.",rightPage:"“That is thoughtful,” Fox replied, “but it would be kinder still to be gentle when people are right here beside you.” Just then, Bear noticed he was standing across the narrow path. He stepped aside, held a low branch so the family could pass, and greeted them with a friendly smile. Fox nodded: Bear’s care was finally in the right place."},
{id:"the-tortoise-and-the-eagle",title:"The Tortoise and the Eagle",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A tortoise begs an eagle to carry her into the sky, then falls to her death when he lets her go.",moral:"Not every wish is safe to pursue; think carefully about what you can do safely.",leftPage:"A tortoise watched sea birds flying and complained that she could never fly. An eagle asked what reward she would offer for a ride. The tortoise promised him all the riches of the Red Sea. The eagle agreed, lifted her high in his talons, and carried her toward the clouds.",rightPage:"Then the eagle let her go. The tortoise fell from the sky onto a rocky mountain and died. In her final moment, she understood that she had asked for something she could not safely do. A tortoise can travel steadily on the ground, but cannot fly among the clouds."},
{id:"the-fox-and-the-goat",title:"The Fox and the Goat",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Think Before You Leap",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A thirsty goat follows a fox into a well, then must find a way back out after the fox leaves without keeping his promise.",moral:"Pause to think before you leap, and remember that trust is built by keeping promises.",leftPage:"One hot afternoon, Fox slipped into an old stone well while chasing a fallen fig. The sides were too smooth for him to climb. Soon Goat came by, thirsty from the dusty path. “Is the water cool?” she asked. “Delightful,” said Fox, hiding his worry. Goat leaned over, lost her footing, and splashed gently into the water beside him.",rightPage:"Fox proposed that Goat brace herself against the wall so he could climb onto her back and reach the rim. He scrambled out, then hurried away without helping. Goat called loudly, and a shepherd nearby lowered a rope and bucket. With his help she climbed to safety. Afterward, she always looked carefully before stepping near a well—and Fox’s promises no longer fooled her."},
{id:"the-raven-and-the-swan",title:"The Raven and the Swan",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Self-Acceptance",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A raven tries to copy a swan’s pale feathers, then discovers that his own gifts are worth keeping.",moral:"You do not need to become someone else to be wonderful; care for the gifts that are already yours.",leftPage:"One bright morning, a raven watched a swan glide across a lake, her white feathers shining in the sun. “Perhaps the water makes her so bright,” he wondered. Hoping to look just like her, he left the familiar fields where he found plenty to eat and settled beside the lake, dipping and rinsing his dark wings again and again.",rightPage:"At last, the raven looked at his reflection and saw that his feathers were still as dark as ever. But he also noticed his own strong wings and clever eyes. A nearby swan showed him where berries grew along the bank, and soon he was enjoying a fine supper. The raven flew home content, glad to be himself and to know a new place, too."},
{id:"my-lord-bag-of-rice",title:"My Lord Bag of Rice",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Courage and kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A fearless traveler helps a dragon king protect his home, and receives a humble gift that proves generous and magical.",moral:"A brave heart and a helpful spirit can bring unexpected blessings.",leftPage:"Long ago in Japan, a traveler named Hidesato reached a bridge where a great dragon lay across the way. Though startled, he stepped carefully past without harming it. The dragon became a courteous man, the ruler of a lake palace, and asked Hidesato for help: a many-legged creature was troubling his family, and he needed a brave friend.",rightPage:"Hidesato went with the lake ruler and waited patiently through the night. When the troublesome creature appeared, he used his bow to drive it away, and the palace grew peaceful again. Grateful, the ruler gave him a bell, a fine cloth, and a small bag of rice that never emptied. Hidesato shared the rice with neighbors, who joyfully called him Lord Bag of Rice."},
{id:"the-tongue-cut-sparrow",title:"The Tongue-Cut Sparrow",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A kind woodcutter searches for the sparrow he loves and discovers that a small mistake can be met with patience, forgiveness, and friendship.",moral:"Kindness and forgiveness can help friends find their way back to one another.",leftPage:"Long ago in Japan, a woodcutter shared his home and supper with a little sparrow. One day, the bird mistook a bowl of rice paste for food and ate it. The woodcutter’s wife, upset about her laundry, shooed the sparrow outdoors before it could explain. When the woodcutter came home and found the bird gone, he set off through the hills to look for his friend.",rightPage:"At the edge of a bamboo grove, the sparrow greeted him with a cheerful chirp and led him to a cozy home among the stalks. Her family welcomed the woodcutter with warm tea and a lively dance. He invited the sparrow to visit again, then told his wife how kindly they had treated him. She felt sorry, and when the sparrow returned, they shared supper together."},
{id:"the-story-of-urashima-taro-the-fisher-lad",title:"The Story of Urashima Taro, the Fisher Lad",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A kind fisher helps a sea turtle and visits a shining palace beneath the waves. When he returns to a changed village, a new friend helps him find a place for his memories and stories.",moral:"A kind deed can lead to unexpected friendships; sharing what we learn can help us feel at home.",leftPage:"One bright morning, Urashima Taro saw children crowding around a small sea turtle. He gently asked them to let it go, and they agreed when he offered his lunch fish in exchange. Taro carried the turtle to the shore and watched it paddle away. The next day, the turtle returned, bowed its head, and invited him to visit its home beneath the waves.",rightPage:"Taro rode safely on the turtle’s broad shell to a shining palace, where friendly sea folk welcomed him with music and pearly fruits. Though he enjoyed their company, he soon missed his village. The turtle carried him home with a keepsake box. Many years had passed, and the houses had changed, but a child from Taro’s family welcomed him and listened to his stories. Together they made a new home by the sea."},
{id:"the-farmer-and-the-badger",title:"The Farmer and the Badger",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A farmer discovers a badger nibbling his crops and learns why the visitor has been sneaking into the field. A truthful conversation leads them to a fair, friendly solution.",moral:"Honesty makes it easier to solve a problem together.",leftPage:"One autumn morning, a farmer found muddy pawprints winding through his vegetable rows. Several turnips had been nibbled, and he worried there would not be enough for winter. That evening he waited beside the garden. A small badger crept out, holding a turnip, then froze when the farmer called gently, “Why are you taking my food?”",rightPage:"The badger lowered his head and admitted his family was hungry after their hillside berries ran dry. The farmer listened, then showed him a patch of fallen vegetables he could share, asking that the growing rows be left alone. The badger agreed and helped gather weeds around the patch. By winter, both neighbors had enough, and neither needed to sneak or worry."},
{id:"the-adventures-of-kintaro-the-golden-boy",title:"The Adventures of Kintaro, the Golden Boy",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"In a mountain clearing, strong Kintaro invites his animal friends to a playful wrestling match. When one round turns unfair, he helps them make things right and enjoy the game together.",moral:"A good friend makes sure everyone gets a fair turn.",leftPage:"In Japan’s Ashigara Mountains, young Kintaro was famous for his strength and his friendship with the forest animals. One bright morning, he and his companions—the bear, deer, monkey, and hare—found a sunny clearing. “Let’s hold a friendly wrestling match,” Kintaro said. The deer offered to judge, while the bear shaped a soft ring in the grass.",rightPage:"The monkey and hare began their round, but soon the monkey tugged the hare’s long ear to gain an advantage. Kintaro gently called, “That isn’t fair play.” The monkey let go and apologized. They tried again, this time keeping their paws to themselves, and laughed as they tumbled softly onto the grass. Kintaro shared rice cakes with everyone, winners and friends alike."},
{id:"the-story-of-princess-hase",title:"The Story of Princess Hase",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A gifted young princess is sent away by a jealous stepmother, but a loyal helper keeps her safe until her father finds her.",moral:"Kindness and loyalty can guide us through difficult times.",leftPage:"Long ago in Nara, Princess Hase was born after her parents prayed at Hase Temple. She grew into a thoughtful girl who loved poems and helped others. One day, her verse helped quiet a rushing river and comfort the emperor. Yet the praise made her stepmother jealous, and she sent Hase to a mountain cottage. The family’s faithful helper, Katoda, chose to stay and keep the girl safe.",rightPage:"When Hase’s father returned, he could not find his daughter and searched the hills. At last, he heard a clear voice reciting a prayer from a little cottage. There was Hase, safe with Katoda. She told her father what had happened, and he brought her home, grateful to the loyal helper. Hase’s kindness and poems were cherished, and she lived happily with her family."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-man-who-did-not-wish-to-die",title:"Sentaro and the Island Where Time Stood Still",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Contentment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Sentaro dreams of a life without end and learns to value the ordinary days he already has.",moral:"A long life is not the same as a happy one; make today worthwhile.",leftPage:"Sentaro had a comfortable home, but one day he began to worry that sickness or death might take him away. He wished he could live forever. At a shrine, he prayed to Jofuku for a way to escape life’s end, then drifted into a vivid dream. A paper crane carried him to an island where no one ever grew old or ill.",rightPage:"At first Sentaro was delighted, but after many years of the same unchanging days, he missed home and wished to return. In his dream, the crane carried him back, and Jofuku’s messenger gently reminded him that endless life would not bring happiness by itself. Sentaro awoke at the shrine, went home, and began working with care, grateful for the days he could share with others."},
{id:"the-bamboo-cutter-and-the-moon-child",title:"The Bamboo-Cutter and the Moon-Child",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Family",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A bamboo-cutter and his wife find a tiny child inside a shining stalk and raise her with loving care. When the Moon-Child’s family calls her home, she leaves with gratitude and keeps her earthly family close in memory.",moral:"Love can make a family, and the care we share stays with us even when we must part.",leftPage:"One evening, a bamboo-cutter spotted a stalk glowing softly among the green leaves. Inside sat a tiny girl, no bigger than his hand. He and his wife welcomed her home, naming her Kaguya, and loved her as their own. She grew bright and kind, filling their little house with laughter and wonder. The old couple no longer felt alone.",rightPage:"As Kaguya grew older, she told her parents she had come from the Moon. One clear night, silver-robed visitors arrived to bring her home. She hugged the bamboo-cutter and his wife, thanked them for every kindness, and promised to remember them. Then she rose gently toward the moon. From that night on, its calm glow reminded the couple that their love had traveled with her."},
{id:"the-mirror-of-matsuyama",title:"The Mirror of Matsuyama",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Family and remembrance",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A daughter cherishes the mirror her mother left behind, believing it helps her see her mother. When her father discovers her secret, he understands the love behind it.",moral:"Remembering someone with love can help their kindness live on in us.",leftPage:"In a quiet village in Matsuyama, a kind mother gave her young daughter a little polished mirror before illness carried her away. “When you miss me, look here and remember my love,” she whispered. The girl treasured it. Years later, when her father returned from a long journey, he found his daughter gazing into the mirror and grew puzzled. He gently asked what she saw there.",rightPage:"“Mother is looking back at me,” the girl said, pointing to the face in the glass. Her father leaned closer, then smiled as he recognized how much his daughter resembled the mother he missed. He understood: the mirror had become a comfort, and his daughter had kept her mother’s loving words close. He sat beside her, and together they shared their favorite memories of her mother."},
{id:"the-goblin-of-adachigahara",title:"The Goblin of Adachigahara",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A lost pilgrim shelters at a lonely cottage, discovers its secret, and escapes the Adachigahara goblin by keeping courage until dawn.",moral:"When something feels wrong, trust your caution, keep moving toward safety, and seek help.",leftPage:"Long ago, on the wide plain of Adachigahara in Japan, a Buddhist pilgrim lost his way as evening fell. A lantern gleamed at a lonely cottage, where an old woman welcomed him beside a warm fire. Before sleeping, she warned him not to open the back room. But a strange rustle and glimmer came from behind its screen, and the curious pilgrim peeked inside.",rightPage:"Among scattered hats and walking staffs, he saw the old woman's shadow stretch into a goblin's shape. He hurried outside, whispering his prayer as the goblin called after him. The plain seemed endless, but he kept going, one steady step at a time. At sunrise, the goblin vanished with the night mist. Safe on the road, the pilgrim gave thanks and chose a brighter path."},
{id:"womens-words-part-flesh-and-blood",title:"The Farmer’s Wife and the Island of Treasure",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Contentment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A patient farmer’s single millet sprout brings him an unexpected chance to prosper. When his jealous older brother follows, he learns that wanting more than he needs can lead him into trouble.",moral:"Being content with enough can be wiser than reaching for everything.",leftPage:"Two brothers shared a home, but their trust grew thin when the older brother’s wife secretly cooked most of the millet seed she was asked to lend. One uncooked grain sprouted in the younger brother’s field. He carefully tended it until it grew tall, and a great bird carried its giant ear across the sea to a bright island of treasure.",rightPage:"The younger brother took only a handful and returned safely, using it to make a good life. Jealous, his older brother followed the bird and gathered far too much. When the bird warned that sunrise was near, he finally listened and let it carry him home. He apologized to his brother, and they learned to share honestly and be thankful for enough."},
{id:"the-three-rhymsters",title:"The Three Rhymsters",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Quick thinking",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"At a birthday feast, a farmer is teased during a rhyme contest until his clever wife turns his odd verse into a playful scene.",moral:"A quick mind can shine in unexpected ways, so treat others with respect.",leftPage:"At a birthday feast, three sisters arrived with their husbands. The physician and the magistrate, proud of their learning, challenged the farmer husband to make a verse about four odd things: something in the sky, a tiger on the ground, scissors on the table, and a stable boy in the room. He stumbled through his words, and the others began to chuckle.",rightPage:"Then the farmer’s quick-witted wife stepped from behind a curtain. “It all makes sense as a little play,” she said: “a moonbeam lights the stage, a painted tiger puppet prowls, scissors trim its paper mask, and the stable boy comes in to drum for the maid’s dance.” The guests praised her clever turn and cheerfully drank their own penalty cups."},
{id:"the-magic-cask",title:"The Magic Cask",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A magical cask brings a family riches, but a pile of caps helps a farmer see that his grandfather needs care more than work.",moral:"Kindness matters more than endless riches; share what you have and let others rest.",leftPage:"A farmer found a great clay cask while digging in his field. He carried it home, and his wife brushed it clean. To their amazement, the cask filled with brushes as quickly as she lifted them out. They sold the brushes and soon had enough to live comfortably. Then a coin slipped from the farmer’s pocket into the cask. At once, brushes vanished, and bright coins began to appear instead. Rich at last, they thought.",rightPage:"One afternoon, Grandfather’s soft red cap slipped into the cask. Out came cap after cap until the room looked like a mountain of bright hats. The farmer saw his tired grandfather laughing beneath them and felt ashamed. He helped him out, apologized, and lifted the coin from the cask. The magic stopped; together they kept enough for their needs, shared the rest, and gave Grandfather a well-earned rest."},
{id:"the-bird-with-nine-heads",title:"The Bird with Nine Heads",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A shepherd helps a princess escape a cliffside cave, and a humble token reveals who truly rescued her.",moral:"Tell the truth, and let kind deeds speak for themselves.",leftPage:"One windy afternoon, a storm swept Princess Mei to a high cliff cave, where a great bird with nine heads kept watch. A young shepherd, An, spotted her and gathered villagers to lower a basket. Mei soothed the bird’s sore wing until all nine heads dozed. Before climbing into the basket, she gave An half of her jade hairpin, promising it would show who truly helped her.",rightPage:"The villagers pulled Mei up, but their boastful leader claimed he had rescued her and left An below. A kind river dragon carried An safely down the cliff, and he reached the palace just as Mei was asked to name her rescuer. She fitted her hairpin to An’s half and told the truth. The leader apologized; Mei returned to thank the bird, and the nine heads waved farewell."},
{id:"the-great-flood",title:"The Great Flood",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A kind boy and his mother shelter a traveler, prepare for a flood, and rescue animals and a stranger as the waters rise.",moral:"Kindness matters most when we offer help to everyone in need.",leftPage:"A kind boy and his mother welcomed a tired old traveler, who became like a grandmother to him. Before leaving, she warned that a great flood was coming, and showed them how to prepare a tiny wooden boat. When strange red marks appeared on the stone lions by the town gate, the boy hurried home. The boat grew large enough to carry them as rain filled the streets.",rightPage:"On the rising water, they gathered a dog, cat, mice, raven, and a drenched swarm of bees into their boat. A frightened man drifted past, and the boy insisted they save him too. When the waters fell, the man claimed their treasure, but the raven returned with a letter from the old traveler, revealing she was a guardian spirit. The judge saw the truth, and the boy, his mother, and their animal friends were free to begin again on dry ground."},
{id:"the-herd-boy-and-the-weaving-maiden",title:"The Herd Boy and the Weaving Maiden",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Keeping Promises",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A herdboy and a sky-weaving maiden become friends, then keep their promise to meet each year across a river of stars.",moral:"Even when life takes us in different directions, a promise and a little kindness can keep friendship bright.",leftPage:"Long ago, a kind young herdboy cared for a golden cow, while far above him a thoughtful Weaving Maiden made shining cloth for the sky. One bright evening, the cow carried him among the clouds. The Maiden greeted him warmly, and they spoke of their different worlds. Before he returned to earth, they promised to meet again and share stories beneath the stars.",rightPage:"When the sky ruler learned of their visits, he set a broad river of stars between the heavens and earth, so each could finish their important work. The herdboy and Maiden missed one another, but kept their promise. Once each year, magpies joined wings to make a bridge across the river. The friends met there, laughed together, and carried fresh stories back to their duties."},
{id:"the-lady-of-the-moon",title:"The Lady of the Moon",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Love",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When Chang’e rises to the moon after tasting a gift of immortality, she finds a gentle way to stay close to the people she loves on Earth.",moral:"Even when we are far apart, love can help us find ways to care for one another.",leftPage:"Long ago, the land grew unbearably hot beneath ten bright suns. The archer Hou Yi sent nine suns safely away, leaving one to warm the fields. Grateful, the Queen Mother gave him a little herb that could grant a very long life. Hou Yi brought it home, hoping to share its wonder with his wife, Chang’e, when the time was right.",rightPage:"One day, Chang’e tasted a tiny part of the herb and began to float gently upward. She drifted all the way to the moon, where she found a quiet palace beneath a fragrant cassia tree. Though she missed Hou Yi, she looked down and saw the fields resting in cool moonlight. Each month, she sent the kindest silver glow she could, and Hou Yi knew she was thinking of him."},
{id:"the-girl-with-the-horses-head-or-the-silkworm-goddess",title:"The Girl Who Brought Silk to Her Village",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A lonely girl’s playful promise leads her white horse to bring her father home. Through a gentle change of heart, she discovers a lasting way to help her family and her village with the gift of silk.",moral:"A kind promise begins with listening and caring for one another.",leftPage:"Long ago in China, a girl lived with her father and a gentle white horse. When her father traveled far away, she missed him so much that she joked to the horse, “If you bring him home, I’ll marry you!” The horse hurried off and returned with her father riding safely on his back. The girl laughed at her foolish promise, and her father thanked the clever horse.",rightPage:"That spring, the horse’s shining mane seemed to glow like moonlit thread. The girl followed its silvery strands to a mulberry grove, where tiny silkworms were making cocoons. She learned to care for them, and soon the village had beautiful silk to weave. In time, people remembered the girl as the Silkworm Goddess, a kind guardian who returned each spring to bless the silkworms and the patient hands that tended them."},
{id:"the-dragon-princess",title:"The Dragon-Princess",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A fisherman discovers a dragon-castle, and the emperor learns that a respectful message and thoughtful gifts can open the way to a generous exchange with the Dragon-Princess.",moral:"A kind request and a thoughtful gift can build trust.",leftPage:"By the Sea of Dongting, a fisherman stumbled into a deep hill tunnel and followed its winding passages until he reached a bright dragon-castle. A small dragon guarded the gate, so the fisherman wisely kept his distance and found his way home. When the emperor heard his account, he asked how to approach the Dragon-Princess, keeper of the sea’s pearls, with respect.",rightPage:"An old guide advised sending a courteous letter and thoughtful gifts, not demands. Two messengers carried jade boxes, fragrant wood, and roasted swallows to the castle. The princess welcomed them, read the emperor’s request, and sent back a handful of shining pearls. A dragon carried the messengers swiftly to the riverbank, and the emperor treasured the pearls—and the lesson in kindness."},
{id:"the-frog-princess",title:"The Frog Princess",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Sia marries the Frog King’s daughter, but his careless temper strains their home. When he learns to listen, apologize, and treat his wife and her frog kin with respect, they find their way back to happiness.",moral:"Respect and a sincere apology can help mend hurt feelings.",leftPage:"Long ago beside the Yangtze River, a boy named Sia learned that the Frog King’s daughter was meant to be his bride. As they grew up, Sia’s family welcomed the kind princess, whose visits brought good fortune. But Sia often teased the frogs around their home, and when his wife asked him to be gentle, he grew cross and sent her away.",rightPage:"Soon Sia saw how lonely the house felt without her. He remembered his wife’s kindness and wished he had listened. When the princess returned, he apologized and promised to treat her and the frogs with care. She forgave him, and they agreed to speak kindly when they disagreed. Their home grew peaceful again, and the Frog Princess stayed close to Sia’s family for many happy years."},
{id:"how-the-rajas-son-won-the-princess-labam",title:"How the Raja’s Son Won the Princess Labam",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Kindness and gratitude",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A prince follows the clue of a talking parrot, and kindness to small and great creatures helps him reach Princess Labam’s shining kingdom.",moral:"Kindness given along the way can return as help when you need it most.",leftPage:"A young Raja’s son was told not to hunt beyond the familiar hills. But there he heard a talking parrot speak of Princess Labam, and curiosity became a brave quest. On the road, he shared food with ants and gently helped a tiger with a sore paw. Each promised to remember his kindness. Later, he cleverly settled a quarrel over four magical gifts and set off on a flying bed.",rightPage:"The flying bed carried him to Labam’s faraway country, where a kind old woman sheltered him. At night, he saw the princess shine like a lamp above the rooftops. With help from his magical gifts, he sent her lovely treasures, and she invited him to meet her openly. He told her of his journey and the friends who helped him; Labam admired his kind heart, and they chose a happy life together."},
{id:"the-lambikin",title:"The Lambikin",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Quick thinking",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A little lambkin promises to share a treat with several hungry animals after visiting his granny. With her help, he finds a clever and friendly way to get home safely.",moral:"A clever idea can help solve a problem, and sharing can turn worry into friendship.",leftPage:"A little Lambikin set off along a sunny path to visit his Granny. A jackal, a vulture, and other hungry animals stopped him and said they hoped to catch him for supper. Lambikin thought quickly: “Let me visit Granny first; she’s making a lovely feast, and there may be a bite for you, too!” Curious about the feast, the animals let him pass.",rightPage:"At Granny’s cottage, Lambikin enjoyed warm porridge and sweet apples. When it was time to go, she tucked him inside a roomy wooden berry drum, which rolled gently down the path. The animals heard it humming and asked if Lambikin was inside. He popped out and invited them to share Granny’s extra treats. They all went back together, laughing, and promised to ask before visiting next time."},
{id:"punchkin",title:"Punchkin",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When the magician Punchkin traps Princess Balna’s family, her brave son journeys to find the hidden source of his power and free them.",moral:"Courage grows stronger when we use it to help others—and kindness can turn allies into friends.",leftPage:"Long ago, clever Princess Balna lived with her family in a palace. A powerful magician named Punchkin trapped them behind enchanted doors, hoping no one could challenge him. Balna’s young son discovered the secret: Punchkin’s strength depended on a tiny green parrot hidden far away, beyond a ring of palm trees and watchful spirits. He set out to find it, though the journey seemed impossible.",rightPage:"On the way, the prince rescued two young eagles from a danger in the forest, and their grateful parents promised to help. With their help, he reached the hidden grove and gently carried the parrot away. Punchkin begged him to return it, but the prince asked him to release everyone and give up his spells. Punchkin agreed; his magic faded, the doors opened, and Balna’s family welcomed the helpful eagles home."},
{id:"the-magic-fiddle",title:"The Magic Fiddle",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Mira’s voice lives on in a magical bamboo fiddle until her family hears her story and makes things right.",moral:"Listening and sharing can help mend hurt feelings.",leftPage:"Long ago, seven brothers lived with their sister, Mira, who cooked while they worked. Their wives unfairly blamed her whenever dinner was late, and Mira felt lonely. One noon, at the village pond, the water shimmered away whenever she lowered her pot. A gentle forest spirit carried her safely into a tall bamboo grove, where she became a voice inside a hollow stalk, waiting to be heard.",rightPage:"Years later, a wandering musician shaped the bamboo into a fiddle. Its tender tune brought Mira’s brothers to tears, and a village headman’s son recognized her voice and listened. When Mira stepped from the fiddle, she told her family how lonely she had been. Her brothers apologized, and the wives promised to share the cooking. Mira returned home gladly, and the fiddle played a cheerful song for everyone."},
{id:"loving-laili",title:"Loving Laili",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Love and care",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Prince Majnun finds the fairy Laili by a lotus lake, and with the help of his loyal friend Husain, begins a new life with her. When danger comes, Laili’s wisdom helps them find a safe way home.",moral:"Care means listening to one another and helping each other through trouble.",leftPage:"Prince Majnun was traveling with his loyal friend Husain when he glimpsed a beautiful fairy, Laili, reflected in a lotus lake. He searched until he found her on the far bank, and the two soon became dear to each other. Husain stayed beside Majnun through every twist of their journey, helping him reach Laili’s garden, where they promised to build a life together.",rightPage:"Later, Laili warned Majnun that a proud ruler in a neighboring garden meant trouble. This time Majnun listened. Together, he and Laili pretended to admire a maze of flowers, then slipped through its winding paths while Husain waited with their horse at the gate. They all rode safely home, where King Dantal welcomed Laili and celebrated the friends who had helped one another."},
{id:"the-soothsayers-son",title:"The Soothsayer’s Son",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Gangazara helps several creatures in need, and their friendship later saves him from an unfair accusation and leads him to unexpected happiness.",moral:"Kindness can make friends in unexpected places, and true friends remember it.",leftPage:"After his father died, Gangazara received a horoscope predicting hard times and a later happiness. On his journey he found a tiger, serpent, and rat trapped in an old well. Though warned to be careful, he helped each climb out, then rescued a goldsmith too. The goldsmith thanked him, while the three animals promised to help whenever he needed them.",rightPage:"Later, the tiger brought Gangazara a royal crown it had found. A greedy goldsmith claimed Gangazara had stolen it, and the prince sent him to prison for ten years. His animal friends made a little tunnel and helped him escape; then they showed the prince the truth. Gangazara was welcomed. At the seashore he met his brother and fainted with joy; a blessing revived him, and soon he married the princess and became king."},
{id:"harisarman",title:"Harisarman",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A neglected man pretends to be a seer, but lucky turns of events lead him to admit the truth and earn trust.",moral:"A lucky guess may impress people, but honesty earns lasting trust.",leftPage:"Harisarman was poor and often overlooked. At a wedding feast, no one remembered to offer him food. Feeling hurt, he told his wife he would pretend to be a wise predictor. Soon, a guest’s horse wandered away. Harisarman confidently said it would be found near the southern road; the searchers found it grazing there, and everyone praised his “magic.”",rightPage:"At court, the king asked Harisarman to find stolen gold. Alone, Harisarman scolded his own tongue for pretending to know magic. Maid Jihva, who had hidden the gold, heard him and thought he knew her secret; she confessed where it was. He showed the king and admitted his lucky guess. Asked to name a frog in a jar, Harisarman murmured “Froggie,” his childhood nickname. The king praised his honesty and offered him useful work."},
{id:"the-charmed-ring",title:"The Charmed Ring",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Kindness and teamwork",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A kind merchant’s son saves three animals and receives a magical ring. When it goes missing, his loyal friends work together to bring it home.",moral:"Kindness can bring unexpected friends, and teamwork helps them solve problems.",leftPage:"A young merchant’s son used his travel money to save a dog, a cat, and a little snake from danger. Though his father was disappointed, the young man cared for his new companions. The snake led him to a kind king, who gave him a charmed ring, a pot, and a spoon to thank him for saving the snake’s life.",rightPage:"The ring could call up a cozy home, while the pot and spoon made a delicious meal. One day, a curious bird carried off the shining ring, and the home vanished. The cat and dog searched together, asking their animal friends for help until they found it. Their master thanked them, spoke to the ring, and welcomed everyone back home."},
{id:"the-talkative-tortoise",title:"The Talkative Tortoise",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Think before you speak",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A chatty tortoise gets a chance to visit a mountain with two duck friends, but must remember their important rule during the journey.",moral:"A thoughtful pause can help us keep a promise and stay safe.",leftPage:"In a quiet Himalayan pond lived Tavi the tortoise, who loved to fill every silence with his stories. Two wild ducks became his friends and invited him to visit their mountain home. They would carry him on a stick, they explained, but he must keep his mouth closed all the way. Tavi promised, though staying quiet felt harder than crossing the sky.",rightPage:"As they flew above fields, curious villagers called up, and Tavi felt words bubbling up: he wanted to explain that he was traveling with friends. He began to open his mouth, then remembered the promise and held tight. The ducks carried him safely to a sunny mountain meadow. There, Tavi thanked them and later told his story one turn at a time. Even the king learned to leave space for others."},
{id:"the-gold-giving-serpent",title:"The Gold-Giving Serpent",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Trust",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A farmer’s family receives a daily gold coin from a serpent in return for a bowl of milk. When the farmer’s son reaches for more than the gift freely offered, the serpent withdraws. Patience and an honest apology help them begin again.",moral:"A gift is best received with gratitude, not greed.",leftPage:"Haridatta worked hard on his little farm, but the harvest was small. One hot afternoon he noticed a serpent resting beside an anthill and left it a bowl of milk as a respectful greeting. The next morning, a gold coin lay in the bowl. This happened day after day. When Haridatta had to visit the village, his son took the milk instead—and began wishing for a whole heap of coins.",rightPage:"The boy leaned toward the anthill, hoping to find the serpent’s hidden treasure, but it slipped away and the bowl stayed empty. When Haridatta returned, he explained that the coins had been a kind gift, never a promise of riches. Together they left fresh milk and a sincere apology. After a pause, the serpent returned and placed one coin in the bowl. The family thanked it, and from then on they accepted each gift patiently."},
{id:"the-farmer-and-the-money-lender",title:"The Farmer and the Money-Lender",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A farmer’s kindness earns him a wish-granting conch, which he uses to bring water and fairness to his village.",moral:"Kindness and sharing can turn even a difficult bargain toward the good of all.",leftPage:"In a sun-baked village, a farmer worked hard yet remained in debt to a money-lender. Hoping to learn how to change his luck, he set out to find Ram. He shared his last little cake with a hungry stranger beneath a tree. The stranger smiled: “I am Ram.” Touched by the farmer’s kindness, Ram gave him a conch shell that could grant a wish when blown just so.",rightPage:"Back home, the money-lender stole the shell, but could not make it work. He returned it only after demanding that every wish bring him twice as much as the farmer. When drought came, the farmer wished for water for the village. Three wells appeared—one for him and two for the lender. Instead of wishing anyone harm, the farmer asked that all three wells be shared. The lender agreed, and the village prospered together."},
{id:"raja-rasalu",title:"Raja Rasalu",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"After growing up in a hidden palace, young Rasalu rides out to meet the world. A small mishap at a village well leads him to make amends—and to find his waiting family.",moral:"Courage shines brightest when it is joined with kindness and a willingness to set things right.",leftPage:"For twelve years, Prince Rasalu grew up in a quiet palace beneath the earth, with a lively colt and a bright parrot for company. When his twelfth year arrived, he longed to see the world above. He rode out, washed at the river as his nurses had taught him, and followed a busy road toward a city, eager to discover what lay beyond his hidden home.",rightPage:"Near a village well, his colt shied at a fluttering scarf, and Rasalu’s shield tipped a row of water pots onto the soft ground. He apologized and helped the startled water-carriers gather the pieces, then sent new pots from the palace stores. Their kind words led him to the king and queen, who welcomed their brave son at last. Rasalu had found the world—and begun by helping its people."},
{id:"the-boy-who-had-a-moon-on-his-forehead-and-a-star-on-his-chin",title:"The Boy Who Had a Moon on His Forehead and a Star on His Chin",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A hidden prince with a moon mark and a star mark finds a way to show who he truly is and bring his family together again.",moral:"Your true worth shines through your actions, even when others cannot see your face or name.",leftPage:"A gardener’s daughter once wished aloud for a child with a moon on his forehead and a star on his chin. Years later, her son was born with those very marks—but jealous palace whispers kept him far from his mother. A kind old dog protected the baby, and, when the boy grew, a clever horse helped him travel safely to another kingdom, where he worked quietly as a grain seller’s helper.",rightPage:"At night, the boy sang in the palace garden, and the youngest princess recognized the gentle tune from a lullaby her mother had taught her. She invited him to a moonlit gathering, where he told his story and showed the shining marks on his face. The king sent for the boy’s mother; they embraced, and the palace welcomed them both. From then on, the boy’s song was heard at every joyful festival."},
{id:"the-demon-with-the-matted-hair",title:"The Demon with the Matted Hair",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A brave prince meets a demon who blocks a forest path. When his weapons tangle in the demon’s matted hair, the prince keeps his wits and finds a peaceful way forward.",moral:"Courage can help us stay calm, think clearly, and choose a peaceful solution.",leftPage:"Long ago, a young prince known as the Five-Weapons Prince was returning home through a deep forest. A demon with a great, tangled mass of hair stepped into the path and warned that no traveler could pass. The prince did not boast or run. He carefully tried each of his five weapons, but every one caught fast in the demon’s thick locks.",rightPage:"Now the prince stood unarmed, yet he kept his voice steady. He pointed out that the demon’s own tangled hair had trapped every weapon, and asked why they should keep struggling when they could simply speak. The demon paused, surprised by the prince’s calm. Together they cleared the path, and the demon promised to let travelers pass safely. The prince continued home, remembering that courage can make room for kindness."},
{id:"lifes-secret",title:"Life’s Secret",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A prince shares the secret of the fish and necklace that protect his life, helping jealousy give way to kindness.",moral:"Honesty and kindness can turn jealousy into trust.",leftPage:"Long ago, a wandering holy visitor came to a palace where Queen Suo wished for a child. He gave her a pomegranate-flower remedy and said her future son, Dalim Kumar, would have a hidden life-secret: a great fish in the palace pond held a little box, and inside it lay a golden necklace. In time, Dalim was born and grew into a cheerful boy who loved his pigeons.",rightPage:"One afternoon, a pigeon flew into Queen Duo’s room, and she asked Dalim to explain his secret before she returned it. He told her honestly. Hearing how precious the fish and necklace were, Duo understood that jealousy had made her unkind. She gave back the bird, and together they asked the king to keep the pond peaceful and safe. From then on, both queens cheered Dalim’s games."},
{id:"phakir-chand",title:"Phakir Chand",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When a prince is separated from his closest friend in a magical underwater palace, the minister’s son uses a clever disguise and a shining jewel to bring them together again.",moral:"A loyal friend thinks creatively and does not give up on someone they care about.",leftPage:"The king’s son and the minister’s son were inseparable friends who set out to explore the wide world. One evening, beside a quiet pond, a shining jewel revealed a path to a palace beneath the water. The king’s son ventured inside, but the path closed behind him. His friend was left on the bank, determined to find a way to bring him home.",rightPage:"In a nearby town, the minister’s son learned that a princess from the water palace was being kept in the king’s palace, and that a wanderer named Phakir Chand had a magic jewel. Disguised as Phakir, he won the jewel with a lively dance, then helped the princess slip away. Together they followed its glow beneath the pond, where the prince welcomed them with joy. The friends returned to the world above side by side."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-rakshasas",title:"The Story of the Rakshasas",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Honesty and kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A thoughtful Brahman and his wife meet a lonely rakshasi in a glittering, empty palace. An honest promise turns a frightening misunderstanding into a welcoming new beginning.",moral:"When we are honest about what we need, kindness can help everyone feel safe and welcome.",leftPage:"A poor Brahman set out to seek work, but followed a shining trail of coins to a quiet palace instead. A beautiful lady welcomed him as her long-lost husband and offered a grand feast. Yet the rooms were strangely empty, and no neighbors came to visit. When he asked why, the lady confessed she was a rakshasi whose nighttime roars had frightened the whole village away.",rightPage:"The Brahman’s wife soon came to find him. She saw that the rakshasi was lonely, but asked her to promise to greet people honestly and never frighten them again. The rakshasi agreed. Together they used some of the palace’s treasure to mend the village road and set out a welcoming feast. Little by little, neighbors returned, and the rakshasi gained friends while the Brahman and his wife went home in peace."},
{id:"the-story-of-swet-basanta",title:"The Story of Swet-Basanta",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Family and kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Separated during a hurried escape, brothers Swet and Basanta find their way back to one another with help from a royal elephant, a kind traveler, and a cheerful calf.",moral:"Kindness and loyalty can help families find their way back to each other.",leftPage:"Swet and younger brother Basanta fled their home after their stepmother grew angry when Basanta rescued his pigeon. They carried a wondrous fish said to bring gems: when its eater cried, pearls appeared; when he laughed, bright jewels fell. In a dark forest, Swet went to find a light, but a royal elephant carried him far away, and the people welcomed him as king. Basanta waited, worried.",rightPage:"Basanta’s tears became pearls, and a passing merchant offered to take him to a nearby town. A cheerful calf there knew the way to the palace and led him to Swet. At the gate Basanta laughed with relief, and bright gems appeared. Swet remembered his brother, embraced him, and sent for their family. Together they left the palace, sharing the treasure and beginning a kinder life."},
{id:"the-evil-eye-of-sani",title:"The Evil Eye of Sani",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When Sani’s challenge brings unexpected troubles, Sribatsa and Chintamani stay hopeful, use their gifts, and find their way home together.",moral:"Hard times are easier to face with courage, kindness, and hope.",leftPage:"Long ago, Sani, the god of difficult times, and Lakshmi, the goddess of good fortune, asked the wise Sribatsa to decide who was greater. He honored Lakshmi with the golden seat, and Sani warned that troubles would follow him for three years. Sribatsa and his wife, Chintamani, set out together, trusting Lakshmi’s promise that things would be right again.",rightPage:"A sudden wonder on their journey swept away their belongings and carried the couple apart. Sribatsa kept going, found a magical cow whose golden gifts bore his name, and used his cleverness to earn an honest post by the river. There he found Chintamani safe aboard a passing boat. Reunited, they returned home, and when Sani’s three years ended, good fortune welcomed them again."},
{id:"the-ghost-brahman",title:"The Traveler and His Ghostly Double",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A traveling Brahman returns to find a ghostly double claiming his home. When the king cannot tell them apart, a young cowherd finds a clever, peaceful way to reveal the truth.",moral:"A thoughtful idea can solve a problem that strength or authority cannot.",leftPage:"A kind but poor Brahman left his Bengali village to earn enough to support his family. One evening, a ghost who looked exactly like him appeared at his door and claimed to be the master of the house. Years later, the real Brahman returned. Neighbors and even the king could not tell the two apart, and the bewildered traveler had nowhere to turn.",rightPage:"A young cowherd who played at being king heard the Brahman’s story and offered to help. At the hearing, he set out a tiny glass bottle and invited each claimant to step inside. The ghost proudly shrank to a speck and slipped through the opening. The cowherd gently corked the bottle, and the ghost admitted the home belonged to the traveler. The family welcomed the Brahman back, while the ghost was sent peacefully on its way."},
{id:"per-gynt",title:"Per Gynt",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"On a dark mountain evening, Per Gynt finds a huge, winding troll blocking the shepherds’ hut. With patience and a lantern, he helps the muddled creature untangle itself and make room for everyone.",moral:"A calm mind and a little kindness can solve a problem better than force.",leftPage:"Long ago, Per Gynt was crossing a Norwegian mountain when dusk covered the path. He hoped to reach a shepherds’ hut before night grew colder, but a huge, winding shape blocked the doorway. “Who are you?” Per called. “I’m the Crooked One,” rumbled a voice. “I’m lost,” it added, “and I can’t tell where I end!”",rightPage:"Per lit his lantern and saw that the Crooked One had looped around the hut by mistake. “Let’s follow the path back to the open hillside,” he suggested. Slowly, the troll unwound, while Per showed him where to turn. The doorway cleared, and the grateful Crooked One helped carry firewood inside. Per and the shepherds welcomed the morning together, safe and warm."},
{id:"the-isle-of-udrost",title:"The Isle of Udröst",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Kindness and Gratitude",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Fisherman Isaac finds a hidden island, makes unexpected friends, and learns that a little help can bring good fortune home.",moral:"When others help you, remember to share your good fortune in return.",leftPage:"Fisherman Isaac was sailing home when fog and wind hid every familiar shore. He kept his small boat steady until, at last, it glided onto a sunny island bright with green hills. A kindly old man in a blue coat welcomed him inside. Isaac soon learned that the man’s three sons, whom he had glimpsed as cormorants at sea, were friendly after all.",rightPage:"The sons invited Isaac fishing, but his hooks brought up nothing. Their father lent him better ones, and soon Isaac had a fine catch to take home. He was given a sturdy boat and later sold fish enough to buy a schooner. Remembering the island’s welcome, Isaac always left a thoughtful gift for the people who watched over boats in winter."},
{id:"the-three-lemons",title:"The Three Lemons",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A cheerful young traveler follows a magical whistle and finds three enchanted lemons. With patience and a little help, he brings three sisters safely back to their home.",moral:"A kind heart and a steady mind can turn a difficult journey into a hopeful one.",leftPage:"Mika was the youngest of three brothers, and people often overlooked him because he liked whittling by the stove. On the road to seek their fortunes, he shared his lunch with a hungry old wanderer. She thanked him and pointed out a silver whistle in the moss. When Mika played it, tiny woodland helpers appeared and showed him a tower where three sisters were enchanted inside golden lemons.",rightPage:"Mika tucked the lemons safely away, then hurried toward a stream when he heard faint calls from within. One by one, he opened them beside the water, and three sisters stepped out, smiling and grateful. He shared his warm cloak with them, and together they followed the helpers home. Their family welcomed them with music and a bright feast, while Mika discovered that kindness had brought him the finest friends of all."},
{id:"the-neighbor-underground",title:"The Neighbor Underground",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A struggling farmer learns why his underground neighbor needs the cow stable moved, and their cooperation brings good fortune to both homes.",moral:"Listening to a neighbor’s needs can turn a problem into a lasting friendship.",leftPage:"Long ago in Telemark, a farmer with little luck built a cow stable on his new patch of land. One morning, a friendly stranger greeted him and pointed to a beautiful farm nearby, hidden beneath the earth. “Your stable stands over my home,” he explained. “Would you move it, so my family can eat without straw and soil falling into our supper?”",rightPage:"The farmer first worried about the cold weather and the work ahead. But after visiting the underground home and seeing the trouble for himself, he agreed to move the stable. Each night, unseen helping hands made the new building rise a little faster. From then on, the farmer’s fields and cattle prospered. In a later lean season, his neighbor secretly led the herd to safe green pasture and brought every animal home healthy in spring."},
{id:"the-troll-wedding",title:"The Troll Wedding",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Trust",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"At a mountain pasture, a clever dog helps a young herder see through a troll-made wedding and find her real sweetheart.",moral:"When something feels wrong, pause, trust your good sense, and seek help from someone you trust.",leftPage:"Long ago, in Norway, a young herder named Liv stayed at a mountain pasture with her loyal dog, Brisk. Her cattle were calm, and she looked forward to marrying her sweetheart. One afternoon a young man who looked just like him arrived, saying they should wed at once. Though friendly guests soon filled the hut, Liv felt puzzled: her dog knew none of them.",rightPage:"Brisk raced down the mountain and led Liv’s real sweetheart back to the hut. He called Liv by name, and the spell began to fade: the guests rolled away as balls of gray yarn, while the feast became moss and mushrooms. Liv recognized her true sweetheart, and he gently helped her home. They celebrated with family in the village, keeping the bright crown as a reminder to trust kind instincts and loyal friends."},
{id:"the-hat-of-the-huldres",title:"The Hat of the Huldres",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A farmhand unknowingly wears a Huldre hat that makes him invisible, then discovers why the wedding guests could not see him.",moral:"A little patience and a friendly greeting can help everyone feel welcome.",leftPage:"On his way to a wedding feast, a farmhand spotted a curious brown strainer in the grass. He tucked it into his coat, thinking it might help at home. At the feast, everyone seemed to pass him by: no greetings, no invitation to eat. Feeling unwanted, he walked home. “I’m back,” he called, though his wife could hear him but could not see him.",rightPage:"His wife noticed the strange strainer on the bench. The moment he set it down, she saw him again—and the little Huldre hat vanished, as if it had only been lent. Now he understood why the guests had not noticed him. He hurried back, returned to the feast, and this time greeted everyone cheerfully. They welcomed him to the table, where he shared a warm meal among friends."},
{id:"the-magic-apples",title:"The Magic Apples",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A young traveler lends his enchanted gifts to a princess, then helps her when a magical mishap leaves her with silver horns.",moral:"Trust grows when we return what we borrow and make things right.",leftPage:"Long ago in Norway, a young traveler named Eirik set out to see the world. A kind old woman gave him a cap that hid him from sight, a cloth that spread a feast, and a purse that never ran empty. At a palace, the princess admired the clever gifts and asked to borrow them; Eirik trusted her, but she kept them.",rightPage:"That winter, the princess ate three strange apples from a traveling peddler, and little silver horns sprouted on her forehead. Eirik returned wearing his hiding cap and offered three antidote apples, one at a time. As each horn shrank, the princess returned his gifts and apologized. The last apple made her forehead smooth; she thanked Eirik, and they shared the feast-cloth's supper with the whole palace."},
{id:"east-of-the-sun-and-west-of-the-moon",title:"East of the Sun and West of the Moon",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A brave girl searches for the prince taken by an enchantment and helps him break free.",moral:"Courage and patience can carry you through a difficult journey.",leftPage:"In a Norwegian valley, a poor family worried about winter. A white bear promised them a warm home if their youngest daughter chose to travel with him. At his shining palace, the bear became a kind prince under an enchantment. Each night he visited her, but she was asked to wait until the spell was broken. Curious, she lit a candle one evening; the light revealed him, and the spell swept him away to a castle beyond the mountains.",rightPage:"The girl asked the winds for help, and the North Wind carried her across snowy peaks to the distant castle. There, the prince was promised to someone else, and a shirt with three wax marks was set out as an impossible test. He recognized the girl and asked her to try. She washed gently, the marks disappeared, and the enchantment broke. They returned together, opening the palace as a warm welcome for weary travelers."},
{id:"kari-woodencoat",title:"Kari Woodencoat",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A kind princess and a helpful blue bull journey through enchanted forests to a new home, where Kari’s identity is finally recognized.",moral:"Kindness and patience can carry us through hard times; a person’s worth is more than their appearance.",leftPage:"Kari was a kind princess, but when her father traveled away, her stepfamily sent her to tend cows and gave her little to eat. A gentle blue bull noticed her tears and shared a magic cloth that spread a warm supper whenever she needed one. Soon the jealous queen discovered their secret, and Kari and the bull set off together, seeking a safe home.",rightPage:"Through three shining forests, Kari accidentally gathered a copper leaf, a silver leaf, and a golden apple. At a faraway castle, she wore a clattering wooden coat and worked in the kitchen. On festival Sundays, a mountain friend gave her bright gowns to wear to church; the prince admired the mysterious visitor, who left behind a golden shoe. When it fit Kari, her true name was known, and she was welcomed as a princess."},
{id:"soria-moria-castle",title:"Soria-Moria Castle",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Halvor finds courage to free three princesses and follows a magical path to the castle where he earns a joyful reunion.",moral:"Courage can help us do difficult things for others; perseverance can lead us home to what matters.",leftPage:"Halvor, once a boy who preferred daydreaming by the hearth to learning a trade, sailed away to see the world. A storm carried him to a distant shore, where he found a shining, empty castle and a princess who warned him of a troll. Though the troll seemed fearsome, Halvor found the courage to use a strength-giving drink and a great sword to free her. Then he set off to rescue her two sisters.",rightPage:"After freeing all three sisters, Halvor returned home, where the firelight helped his parents recognize their long-lost son. The youngest princess gave him a ring, but soon the sisters were far away again. Halvor kept searching: an old woman lent him swift boots, and the West Wind carried him toward Soria-Moria Castle. At the wedding feast, he showed the ring, and the youngest princess chose him to share her happy new life."},
{id:"connla-and-the-fairy-maiden",title:"Connla and the Fairy Maiden",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Family",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Connla meets a fairy maiden who offers him a wondrous journey. He discovers that choosing wonder need not mean forgetting the people he loves.",moral:"New adventures are wonderful, and love helps us find our way home.",leftPage:"One bright morning, Connla wandered along a green hill above the sea. A maiden in a blue cloak appeared, carrying a silver apple that never grew smaller, no matter how many bites she took. She told him of a shining island where music drifted through the trees. Connla longed to see it, but he thought of his father waiting at home.",rightPage:"The maiden held out her hand, and a little boat glided to shore. Connla sailed with her across calm, sparkling water, then saw his home far behind. He asked to return and tell his father of the island. The maiden smiled, turned the boat around, and brought him safely back. Connla shared the apple’s last sweet bite with his father, then promised to visit the island another day."},
{id:"guleesh",title:"Guleesh",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A Mayo boy joins a fairy ride to France, then helps a worried princess find safety and her way home.",moral:"Courage can begin with noticing when someone needs help and listening to what they want.",leftPage:"In County Mayo, Guleesh wished for a life beyond his quiet home. One moonlit night, he followed merry fairy riders into a grassy hill and found himself racing across the sea. In France, the fairies planned to carry away a princess from her wedding feast. Seeing how unhappy and frightened she looked, Guleesh decided he must help her get somewhere safe.",rightPage:"When the fairies whisked the princess away, Guleesh helped her onto his horse and rode with her back to Ireland. He brought her to a kind priest, who sheltered her while Guleesh searched for a way to help. A strange healing herb restored her voice. She chose to return to her family, and Guleesh was thanked for listening and helping her find her own way."},
{id:"the-field-of-boliauns",title:"The Field of Boliauns",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Tom catches a leprechaun who offers to show him hidden gold. But when a clever trick makes every plant look like the marked one, Tom has to decide what matters more: a pot of coins or keeping his promise.",moral:"A little greed can muddle good judgment; honesty can bring peace of mind.",leftPage:"One bright morning, Tom Fitzpatrick spotted a tiny leprechaun beside a field of yellow boliauns. Tom gently asked where the little fellow kept his gold, and the leprechaun offered to show him. “It’s beneath that flower,” he said, pointing to one plant. Tom tied his blue ribbon around its stem, then hurried home to fetch a small spade.",rightPage:"When Tom returned, the field shimmered with blue ribbons: the leprechaun had marked every boliaun just alike! Tom laughed at the clever trick. He could spend all day digging, but he had promised to let the leprechaun go. So he untied the little fellow, who skipped away, and Tom walked home content, glad he had kept his word rather than chased uncertain treasure."},
{id:"the-horned-women",title:"The Horned Women",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A wool-worker welcomes a curious group of horned visitors, then uses a well’s clever advice to solve their puzzling request and send them home peacefully.",moral:"When a task seems impossible, pause, listen, and look for a clever solution.",leftPage:"One rainy evening in Ireland, Nóra was carding wool beside the hearth when a traveler with one tiny horn knocked. Nóra welcomed her in. Soon more horned visitors arrived, each carrying a spindle and asking to join the work. They spun quietly, then asked Nóra to bake a cake. “Bring water in this sieve,” they said, handing her one with wide holes.",rightPage:"At the well, Nóra sighed as every drop slipped away. A gentle voice from the water suggested she seal the holes with soft clay and moss; then she carried water home. At the well’s advice, she called, “The hill and sky shine bright!” The visitors hurried back to their mountain. Nóra closed the door, tucked the wool safely away, and shared warm cake with her family, glad she had listened and thought carefully."},
{id:"conall-yellowclaw",title:"Conall Yellowclaw",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Conall and his three sons set out to find a king’s prized brown horse. A clever plan gets them into the stable, but honesty and compassion bring them safely home with the horse.",moral:"Courage matters, and honesty can open a kinder path than force.",leftPage:"When Conall’s sons made a serious mistake, a neighboring king gave their father a difficult choice: bring back the brown horse of Lochlann, or lose his sons. Conall gathered the three young men and crossed the sea. The horse’s king guarded the stable closely, so Conall asked the royal miller for help and devised a quiet way inside.",rightPage:"Hidden among sacks of bran, Conall and his sons reached the stable, but the lively horse made such a racket that the king discovered them. Conall admitted why they had come and asked forgiveness. Seeing a father trying to save his family, the king chose kindness: he let them take the brown horse home. The Irish king welcomed them, and the family began again in peace."},
{id:"hudden-and-dudden-and-donald-oneary",title:"Hudden and Dudden and Donald O’Neary",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A poor farmer turns a trick into a lesson for two greedy neighbors, who finally learn to share.",moral:"Cleverness is best when it helps people mend their ways and live more kindly.",leftPage:"Long ago in Ireland, Donald O’Neary lived beside two wealthy farmers, Hudden and Dudden, who envied his little patch of grass. His gentle cow, Daisy, grew old and died peacefully. Donald carried her hide to market. Before entering an inn, he tucked pennies into tiny slits in it, then let them tumble out as if the hide could make money. A trader paid him a purse of gold for this wondrous hide.",rightPage:"Hudden and Dudden copied Donald, but their trick failed at market, and they went home empty-handed. Angry, they planned to carry Donald to the Brown Lake in a sack. Donald persuaded them to look into its shining water: a meadow full of cattle seemed to lie below. As they leaned closer, they saw their own reflections and stopped. Donald explained the joke and shared pasture; the farmers apologized, and they became friends."},
{id:"munachar-and-manachar",title:"Munachar and Manachar",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Sharing",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When Manachar eats all the berries, Munachar follows a chain of curious requests to gather what they need. The two friends make amends and share their next harvest.",moral:"When we share and help each other, a small problem can become a happy beginning.",leftPage:"Long ago, Munachar and Manachar went berry picking. Munachar filled his basket, while Manachar ate every berry he picked. “I need help before we have nothing left!” Munachar said, and set off to find a rod for a berry basket. But the rod asked for an axe, the axe needed a sharpening stone, and the stone needed water. The water led him to a deer, then a hound, and each helper asked for something else.",rightPage:"At the river, Munachar dipped the sieve, but water slipped through. A crow called, “Seal the holes with clay!” Munachar did, carried water to the miller, and soon the helpers passed along flour, straw, milk, and cream. He hurried back expecting a quarrel, but Manachar was waiting, sorry and ready to plant more raspberry bushes. Together they baked a cake and promised to share the next harvest."},
{id:"king-otoole-and-his-goose",title:"King O’Toole and His Goose",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Promises",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When age leaves King O’Toole unable to hunt, a visitor helps his old goose regain her strength. The king keeps his promise and finds a new way to enjoy his land.",moral:"A promise matters, and sharing what you have can bring joy to many.",leftPage:"Long ago in Ireland, King O’Toole loved riding over the hills, but as he grew older he could no longer go hunting. His dear goose kept him company, swimming for fish and circling the lake, until she too became tired and could hardly fly. One morning the king met a kind stranger named Kavin, who offered to restore the goose’s strength in return for the land she flew over.",rightPage:"The king agreed, and Kavin gently lifted the goose and sent her gliding into the clear sky. She soared around the lake, over the green fields, and back to her friend, bright-eyed and strong again. King O’Toole gladly kept his promise, giving Kavin the land she had crossed. Kavin made it a welcoming place for travelers, while the king and goose enjoyed many happy days together."},
{id:"salmon-of-knowledge-fionn",title:"The Salmon of Knowledge",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Ask About Ireland, ‘The Salmon of Knowledge’",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Young Fionn helps the poet Finnegas cook a magical salmon said to hold all the world's knowledge. When Fionn accidentally burns his thumb and tastes it while cooling the burn, he becomes the one destined to receive the salmon's wisdom.",moral:"Curiosity and attentive learning can open the way to wisdom; share what you learn generously.",leftPage:"Long ago, young Fionn went to learn from Finnegas, a wise poet beside the River Boyne. Finnegas knew many things, but he was still searching for the Salmon of Knowledge, a magical fish said to hold all the world's wisdom. At last, he caught it and hurried home, asking Fionn to cook it carefully.",rightPage:"Finnegas warned him not to eat a bite, then stepped away for firewood. While turning the fish, Fionn burned his thumb and put it in his mouth to soothe it. Finnegas saw a new light in the boy's eyes: the prophecy had come true. He told Fionn to eat the fish and press that thumb to his lips again. Wisdom flooded Fionn's mind, and he grew to be a great leader."},
{id:"the-brewery-of-eggshells",title:"The Brewery of Eggshells",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When a worried mother hears a strange voice comment on her tiny eggshell pot, she learns how to resolve a suspected fairy mix-up gently.",moral:"Curiosity and kindness can solve worries more wisely than fear.",leftPage:"Long ago in Ireland, a farmer and his wife watched their twin babies closely. Though the babies were fed and cuddled, they seemed not to grow, and the parents worried that some fairy trick had happened. A wise neighbor told the mother to set a tiny eggshell pot by the hearth and listen carefully; sometimes, she said, an unusual visitor would reveal itself.",rightPage:"As the mother pretended to stir the little pot, one baby sat up and spoke in a voice far older than its face: “I have seen forests rise from acorns, but never a brewery made in an eggshell!” The mother understood, yet chose not to frighten anyone. She called kindly to the fair folk, and they came to exchange babies peacefully. Soon both families had their own little ones safe at home."}
];
